  "sections": [
    {
      "contentId": 17592186097832,
      "id": "bd9397e1-06d5-59ff-a830-f9894033fdaf",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ shown in the picture below. The vector $2\\mathbf{a}$ is the vector that has the same direction as $\\mathbf{a}$ and a length (magnitude) that is twice the length of $\\mathbf{a}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n|2\\mathbf{a}|=2 \\cdot |\\mathbf{a}|\n$$\n\n![](images/01/01c393b2df3d39e4f62faca475a26ea835e1ab88266628509d58e21edd36c79b.png)\n\nOn the other hand, $-1.5\\mathbf{a}$ is the vector that has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{a}$ and its magnitude is $1.5$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$: \n\n$$\n|-1.5\\mathbf{a}|=1.5 \\cdot |\\mathbf{a}|\n$$\n\n![](images/73/73ea353af74ab9518ea7a963985ad26ad36723c7e3a335391628f1eba208139e.png)\n\nNotice that $2\\mathbf{a}$ and $-1.5\\mathbf{a}$ are both parallel to $\\mathbf a,$ and are parallel to each other.",
      "mathAcademyId": "2905",
      "stepId": "17592186097478",
      "title": "Introduction"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186096446,
      "id": "f0e71ce6-22dd-5638-81d3-c222aada5c61",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186096649,
        "title": "Creating Parallel Vectors"
      },
      "markdown": "The vector $\\mathbf a$ is shown in the diagram below. Draw the vectors $2\\mathbf a$ and $-3\\mathbf a$.\n\n![](images/a5/a5eaa8baaf303ecbfa6c0fb207d6b19836cc90fc331e238cd473109f44fddf80.png)",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-4280",
      "stepId": "17592186097480",
      "title": "Creating Parallel Vectors",
      "workedSolution": "First, notice that both $2\\mathbf{a}$ and $-3\\mathbf{a}$ must be parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nNow, for the vector $2\\mathbf{a}$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $2\\mathbf{a}$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $2\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction since the factor $2$ is positive.\n\n![](images/41/41eba7e6c5bbe420418726bd45efa668dc6be5ff5144ba35d52b53a43925c333.png)\n\nNext, for the vector $-3\\mathbf{a}$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $3\\mathbf{a}$ is $3$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $-3\\mathbf{a}$ have opposite directions since the factor $-3$ is negative.\n\n![](images/ed/eda3f2572d98ef1789292ec6d35569487b32efa459673dcb95f8fdd0d1f4ae5b.png)"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186097852,
      "id": "afa24342-fd53-5361-8f24-8c57d1a13102",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "In general, two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are called **parallel** (or **collinear**) if there exists a number $\\lambda$ such that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\lambda \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSometimes, we'll use a special notation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\parallel \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nto denote that $\\mathbf{a}$ is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$.",
      "mathAcademyId": "3401",
      "stepId": "17592186097562",
      "title": "Determining Whether Two Vectors Are Parallel"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186096445,
      "id": "993ef18e-6262-5636-9d9a-4e0c91fc4973",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186096648,
        "title": "Determining Whether Two Vectors are Parallel"
      },
      "markdown": "Suppose, $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$. Given that $7\\mathbf{a}+14\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\lambda \\left(3\\mathbf{a}+6\\mathbf{b} \\right)$ are equal vectors, find the scalar factor $\\lambda$.",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-4279",
      "stepId": "17592186097479",
      "title": "Determining Whether Two Vectors are Parallel",
      "workedSolution": "Notice that\n\n$$\n7\\mathbf{a}+14\\mathbf{b}= \\dfrac{7}{3} (3\\mathbf{a}+6\\mathbf{b})\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lambda=\\dfrac{7}{3}\n$$"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186096494,
      "id": "cbf661ea-7d87-5c8d-8bee-63a814c037dc",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186096700,
        "title": "Identifying Parallel Vectors From a Diagram"
      },
      "markdown": "Consider the diagram that is shown below. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{b}=-\\mathbf{c}$\n2. $\\mathbf{e}=2\\mathbf{a}$\n3. $\\mathbf{a}=2\\mathbf{e}$\n\n![](images/95/95ae711c1ccbf7543e6d269aa8d817a66e2ac348113ea71f3a1babcbb23d1ca3.png)",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-5183",
      "stepId": "17592186097563",
      "title": "Identifying Parallel Vectors From a Diagram",
      "workedSolution": "- Statement I is false. The vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ have different magnitudes. So, $\\mathbf{b} \\neq -\\mathbf{c}$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{e}$ are parallel and have the same direction. Also, the magnitude of $\\mathbf{e}$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$. So, $\\mathbf{e}=2\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Statement III is false. The magnitude of $\\mathbf{e}$ is greater than the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$. So, $\\mathbf{a} \\neq 2\\mathbf{e}$.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186097834,
      "id": "a19c5fdc-f46e-5a71-be1f-0f7a02107fb3",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "For any vector $\\mathbf{a}$ the vector $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{a}$ is equal to the zero vector: \n\n$$\n0\\cdot\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to *any* vector.",
      "mathAcademyId": "2907",
      "stepId": "17592186097481",
      "title": "The Zero Vector is Parallel to Any Vector"
    }
  ],
